The 400 is from a type conversion error: the request `GET /api/cardio/Jog` is matching your `GET /api/cardio/{id}` handler, Spring tries to convert `"Jog"` to a numeric `id` and fails (Bad Request). Use the name-specific path.

Change the GET in `backend/src/test/java/dev/jclawlor/FitnessGoals/controller/CardioExerciseControllerTest.java` to call the name endpoint:

```java
// java
@Test
@Transactional
public void testgetCardioExerciseByName() throws Exception{
    final CardioExerciseDto cardioDto = new CardioExerciseDto("Jog", 20.0, 200, 10.0);
    final CardioExerciseDto savedCardioDto = cardioService.addCardioExercise(cardioDto);

    // Use the name-specific route to avoid hitting the /{id} endpoint
    mvc.perform(get("/api/cardio/name/" + savedCardioDto.getName()))
        .andDo(print()).andExpect(status().isOk())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Jog"))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.durationMinutes").value(20))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.caloriesBurned").value(200))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.pace").value(10));

    mvc.perform(get("/api/cardio/name/NonExistent")).andDo(print()).andExpect(status().isNotFound());
}
```

The 500 occurs because your service throws a plain `RuntimeException` when the name isn't found, which propagates and becomes a 500. Return a 404 instead — either throw a `ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ...)` from the service or have the controller return `ResponseEntity.notFound()` when the repository `Optional` is empty.

Change in `backend/src/main/java/dev/jclawlor/FitnessGoals/service/impl/CardioExerciseServiceImpl.java` — replace the `orElseThrow` `RuntimeException` with `ResponseStatusException`:

